{"reference":"John 10","book":"John","chapter":10,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"“I and the Father are one”","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Jesus’ statement in verse 30 comes after he speaks of his sheep’s safety in both his hand and the Father’s hand, and it is immediately followed by debate over his works, his authority, and the charge of blasphemy. Read first in that setting: the saying belongs to the chapter’s intertwined claims about shepherding, protection, the Father’s works, and Jesus’ agency. The chapter’s earlier “I am” statements—“I am the door” and “I am the good shepherd”—also use images that characterize Jesus’ role; such sayings should be read in their particular contexts rather than treated as identical kinds of claim. Later Trinitarian reception of verse 30 is important, but it should be distinguished from the immediate argument of John 10.","verse_refs":["John 10:22-39","John 10:7-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_010_7732f73de14594220b6d8242"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}